        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>Is the Matrix T5x a commercial treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Yes. The T5x is a Matrix club treadmill with a 5.0 HP AC drive, incline to 15% and a 60-inch running length, built for gyms. Matrix’s manual specifies a dedicated 20-amp circuit. It is not on Matrix’s US home store, and most US buyers now find it remanufactured by specialist resellers.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How much is a used Matrix T5x worth?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              On 14 October 2026 a US remanufacturer listed the older-style T5x at $3,999 and the newer-style at $4,399, with a one-year parts and on-site labour warranty. As-is units from gym clear-outs and private sellers go for much less, usually without warranty or delivery. Price an as-is unit as one that may need a belt and a technician.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What is the weight limit on the Matrix T5x?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Sources disagree. Matrix’s 2006 MX-T5x manual gives 500 lb, while reseller listings for current remanufactured units give 400 lb. The name has covered more than one generation, so check the frame label and confirm the rating for that build with Matrix or a service company. Without confirmation, assume 400 lb.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can I plug a Matrix T5x into a normal outlet?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Usually not. Matrix’s manual specifies a 20-amp supply with a NEMA 5-20 plug on 120 volts, which has one sideways blade and does not fit a standard 15-amp outlet. You need a dedicated 20-amp circuit with the matching receptacle, installed by an electrician if necessary. Do not use an adaptor or extension cord.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is the Matrix T5x part of the Matrix recall?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Not in any recall we found. The 2022 and 2024 recalls cover Matrix T1x, T1xe, T3x, T3xe, T3xh and T3xm commercial treadmills, and the January 2026 expansion covers retail T30, TF30, T50, TF50 and T75 models in stated serial ranges, all for a loose power cord. Check cpsc.gov if you are looking at any of those.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What is the difference between the older and newer Matrix T5x?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              One remanufacturer sells both. Its chart lists the same 5.0 HP AC drive, 0.5 to 12 mph and 15% incline for each, with the newer style having a dot-matrix LED console and more programmes, including military fitness tests. Dimensions and weights differ slightly. Width and weight ratings conflict across sources, so confirm them for the unit you buy.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

